in its formal submission to the office  of the prosecutor at the international  criminal court Al Jazeera included a  dossier of evidence that its lawyers say  cannot be ignored the submission to the  ICC prosecutor has highlighted the new  witness evidence and video footage which  clearly shows that Shireen and her  colleagues were directly fired at by  Israeli Defense Forces  the evidence they say undermines  Israel's claim that journalist Shireen  Abu akle was shot and killed by accident  caught in crossfire between Israeli  soldiers and Palestinians the lawyers  say it leaves no doubt there was no  Palestinian gunfire Shireen and her  colleagues were clearly identifiable as  journalists going about their work in  the occupied West Bank City of Janine  when they came under direct Israeli  gunfires  the dossier is comprehensive the  evidence is compelling there's no reason  now why the investigation cannot be  undertaken by the ICC the witnesses who  we've located are readily available to  the prosecutor there or here in The  Hague  the crime scene can be investigated and  the whole picture can be put together  and if there's sufficient evidence that  we say the years then the perpetrators  can be charged Al jazeera's submission  to the ICC has sparked a furious  response from the Israeli government  this is an incident that has been  investigated deeply and confidentially I  suggest the officials and Al Jazeera go  and check what happens to journalists in  Iran there is no Army that behaves  morally like the IDF  a news conference at the Hague heard  from International media Representatives  who said shireen's killing was part of a  systematic attempt to silence  journalists voices that would continue  unless Justice was served also present  with shireen's niece Lina abuakle it's  in these moments where we rely on the  International Community and  international courts to investigate such  crimes so so there's accountability and  also to prevent future crimes like this  from going unpunished it's easy to  forget that among the often heated  political rhetoric is a family grieving  we didn't just lose a journalist we lost  a human we lost someone  who was part of our family and who was  my only aunt  there's been no response yet from the  ICC either to the Abu akla family's own  submission of evidence in September this  year or to Al jazeera's case presented  here early on Tuesday but such is the  accumulated weight of evidence the  broadcasters lawyers say that there can  be no reason for the court not to take  the next step and launch a full  investigation  Al Jazeera at The Hague
 

